## Testing strategy

- One Vitest lane (jsdom). `obsidian` resolves to the test double in
  `tests/__mocks__/obsidian.ts`; `tests/setup.ts` polyfills Obsidian's
  `HTMLElement` helpers (`createEl`, `empty`, …). Extend both as you use more
  API surface.
- Services get plain unit tests.{{componentTesting}}
- The mock `Modal` attaches to `document.body` on open, so dialog flows are
  testable with DOM queries; the mock `Notice` logs to `__noticeLog`.
- TDD: failing test first in the mirrored `tests/` path, then the
  implementation, then refactor.

## Adding a feature (checklist)

1. Model state/events first: extend settings (`migrateSettings` keeps old vaults
   safe) and `AppEvents` if other surfaces must react.
2. Put logic in `src/core/` (unit-tested), rendering in `src/ui/`.
3. Register commands in `src/commands.ts` via `plugin.commands`; register
   views/listeners in their `register*` module.
4. User feedback goes through `plugin.notices` / `plugin.modals`; wrap fallible
   async work (I/O, network) in `plugin.errors.run` / `plugin.errors.wrap`.
5. Style via `src/styles.css` (Obsidian CSS variables, no `!important`) or
   scoped SFC styles.
6. Run the local gate set before pushing (see `CLAUDE.md`).

## Lifecycle & cleanup (Obsidian rules)

Let Obsidian own teardown — it auto-cleans anything registered through the
plugin, and manual cleanup is where leaks and layout bugs come from.

- **Register, don't hand-roll.** Obsidian events → `this.registerEvent(...)`
  (see `registerExtras.ts` for the `editor-menu` handler); DOM listeners →
  `this.registerDomEvent(el, type, cb)` (see `statusBar.ts`);
  timers → `this.registerInterval(window.setInterval(...))`; commands/views via
  `addCommand`/`registerView`. All are removed on unload — a raw
  `addEventListener`/`setInterval`/`vault.on()` leaks past unload.
- **Don't detach leaves in `onunload()`.** On update, Obsidian reinitializes
  open leaves at their position; detaching them breaks the user's layout. Just
  register the view type. (The scaffold has no `onunload` for this reason.)
- **Don't store view instances.** Look them up on demand —
  `app.workspace.getLeavesOfType(TYPE)` (see `registerViews.ts`) — never keep a
  `this.view = leaf.view` reference (stale state + leaks).
- **Use `this.app`, not the global `app`/`window.app`** (debug-only), and reach
  the active view via `app.workspace.getActiveViewOfType(...)`, not
  `workspace.activeLeaf`.

## Files & paths

- `normalizePath()` every constructed/user-supplied vault path (cross-platform).
- Prefer the Vault API (`vault.getFileByPath`, `vault.process`,
  `fileManager.processFrontMatter`) over the Adapter API and over iterating all
  files; use the Editor API for the active note.

## Constraints worth remembering

- {{mobileLine}}
- Also avoid regex lookbehind (unsupported on some mobile engines).
- Build artifacts (`main.js`, `styles.css`) are generated — never edit or
  commit them; `src/styles.css` is the stylesheet source.
- `manifest.json`/`versions.json` change only through `npm version` (the
  `sync-version` script) — `check:artifacts` fails on desync. Release tags must
  equal the version with **no** leading `v` (`.npmrc` sets
  `tag-version-prefix=""`).
- Ratchets (`check:loc`, `check:css`, `check:quality`, coverage floors) only
  tighten: lock improvements in with each gate's `--update` in the same PR.
